Problem Statement
You are given a stack of integers. Your task is to reverse the elements of the stack using recursion.

Rules & Constraints:

You must reverse the stack using recursion only.

You are not allowed to use any loop constructs (e.g., for, while).

You are not allowed to use any extra auxiliary data structures (like arrays, queues, strings, or another stack) to hold the elements.

You may only use standard stack operations:

push(): Insert an element at the top.

pop(): Remove the top element.

top() / peek(): View the top element.

empty() / isEmpty(): Check if the stack is empty.


Examples
Example 1

Input Stack: [1, 2, 3, 4] (where 4 is the top element and 1 is the bottom element)

Output Stack: [4, 3, 2, 1] (where 1 is the top element and 4 is the bottom element)

Explanation: The stack is completely reversed. The element that was at the bottom (1) is now at the top, and the top element (4) is now at the bottom.

Example 2

Input Stack: [3, 2, 1, 7, 6] (where 6 is the top element)

Output Stack: [6, 7, 1, 2, 3] (where 3 is the top element)

Explanation: Reversing the stack perfectly flips the order of all elements without using any extra arrays or queues.

The code relies on two recursive functions. The main function pulls out all the elements until the stack is empty. As the recursion unwinds, it uses a helper function to push each held element to the very bottom of the stack.

class Solution {
private:
    //Helper function to insert an element at the very bottom of the stack 
    void insertAtBottom(stack<int>& s, int element) {
        //Base case: If the stack is empty, we have reached the bottom 
        //We can now push our element 
        if (s.empty()) {
            s.push(element);
            return;
        }

        //If the stack is not empty, we need to remove the current top out of way 
        int topElement = s.top();
        s.pop();

        //Recursively dig down to the bottom of the stack 
        insertAtBottom(s, element);

        //Once the element is placed at the bottom, push the temporarily held elements back on top 
        s.push(topElement);
    }

public:
    //Main function to reverse the stack 
    void reverseStack(stack<int>& s) {
        //Base case: If the stack is empty, stop recursing 
        if (s.empty()) {
            return;
        }

        //Hold the top element in the current function frame and remove it 
        int topElement = s.top();
        s.pop();

        //Recursively reverse rest of the remaining stack
        reverseStack(s);

        //Insert the held element at the very bottom of the newly reversed stack 
        insertAtBottom(s, topElement);
    }
};

//Example usage 
int main() {
    stack<int> s;
    s.push(1);
    s.push(2);
    s.push(3);
    s.push(4); //currently 4 is at the top

    Solution obj;
    obj.reverseStack(s);

    cout << "Reversed stack (top to bottom): ";
    while (!s.empty()) {
        cout << s.top() << " ";
        s.pop();
    }
    //Output will be: 1 2 3 4

    return 0;
}

TC: O(N^2)
The reverseStack function makes N recursive calls to empty the stack
Every time it unwinds it calls the helper function insertAtBottom
The insertAtBottom function also takes O(K) time where K is the current size of stack
coz it has to recursively pop all elements to reach the bottom.

This creates a series of operations: 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + N which in math sums up to N(N+1)/2
This leaves us the TC of O(N^2)

SC: O(N)
Recursion uses memory via systems internal call stack.

